# Grey heron

The grey heron is the common large heron of Europe and temperate Asia, a
familiar sight standing motionless at the edge of a river or a fish pond. It
hunts by waiting: the birds stand in shallow water for long periods and catch
fish with a downward stab of the bill, taking eels, roach and perch as they pass
within range. Frogs, voles and ducklings are caught as readily when fish are
scarce, and a heron will hunt a field far from water. It nests colonially in
tall trees, returning to the same heronry for decades. See [[ardeidae]].
